{{#> layouts/base head-sub-title="Modules" page-title="Simple Java Mail Modules" }}

	{{#*inline "html-head-block"}}
<meta name="Description" content="List of all Simple Java Mail modules and what 3rd party dependencies they bring">
	{{/inline}}

	{{#*inline "header-block"}}
		{{> includes/header topic="modules" }}
	{{/inline}}

  {{#*inline "body-block"}}
  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Core already covers well-formed MIME, reusable configuration and message rules, TLS and OAuth2, diagnostics, conversion, and simple batches.
      Add an optional module only when you need pooled or clustered delivery, authenticated SOCKS, DKIM, S/MIME, Outlook conversion, command-line use,
      Spring integration, or Karaf support.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="core-module" class="with-back-to-top">Core</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="Core capabilities">
    <a href="features.html#section-basic-usage"><span>Compose</span><strong>Messages and recipients</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="features.html#section-attachments"><span>Content</span><strong>Attachments and images</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="security.html#section-authentication-methods"><span>Transport</span><strong>TLS and OAuth2</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="features.html#section-converting"><span>Interoperate</span><strong>EML and MIME conversion</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        The main <code class="inline language-text">simple-java-mail</code> dependency already includes the core module. Add any optional feature modules
        alongside it as you need them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Integration modules such as Spring already depend on the main library, so you do not need to declare it twice. Feature modules can be combined.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top: 1em">The main core dependencies are:</p>
      <ul class="indent">
        <li><code>jakarta.mail:jakarta.mail-api</code>: the standard mail API</li>
        <li><code>org.eclipse.angus:angus-mail</code>: the Jakarta Mail implementation</li>
        <li><code>com.sanctionco.jmail:jmail</code>: address validation</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Use the published POM on Maven Central as the authoritative list of transitive dependencies for the current release.</p>
      <p style="margin-top: 1em">
        As of 9.0.0, the <code class="inline language-text">simple-java-mail</code> and <code class="inline language-text">core-module</code> jars include
        Java module descriptors. Modular applications can require
        <code class="inline language-java">org.simplejavamail</code> directly.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
	    <img data-src="../../assets/module_architecture.png"
	         data-src-webp="../../assets/module_architecture.png.webp"
			 alt="Simple Java Mail module overview">
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2 id="batch-module" class="with-back-to-top">Batch and clustering support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="Batch module capabilities">
    <a href="features.html#section-sending-asynchronously"><span>Schedule</span><strong>Asynchronous sending</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="smtp-connection-pooling.html"><span>Choose</span><strong>Pool orchestration options</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="configuration.html#section-clustering"><span>Route</span><strong>Multiple SMTP clusters</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="configuration.html#section-mailer-lifecycle"><span>Close</span><strong>Mailer lifecycle</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
    <p>
      Add this library for pooled and clustered sending:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;batch-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      This adds the following 3rd party dependencies:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://github.com/simple-java-mail/smtp-connection-pool">simple-java-mail:smtp-connection-pool</a></li>
    </ul>
	  <p class="wide">
      The module sends work asynchronously and orchestrates the optional <code>smtp-connection-pool</code> dependency. Use the
      <a href="smtp-connection-pooling.html">pooling and batch orchestration guide</a> to compare the full Mailer integration, the standalone
      callback facade, direct leases, and the Jakarta Mail or Camel provider paths. See
      <a href="configuration.html#section-batch-and-clustering">Mailer configuration</a> for its settings.
    </p>
	  <p class="wide">
		  A live connection pool keeps the JVM running. See <a href="configuration.html#section-mailer-lifecycle">Mailer lifecycle and resource ownership</a>
		  for orderly shutdown, including clustered Mailers.
	  </p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="authenticated-socks-module" class="with-back-to-top">Authenticated (SOCKS) proxy support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="Authenticated SOCKS module capabilities">
    <a href="features.html#section-proxy"><span>Send</span><strong>Authenticated SOCKS</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="configuration.html#section-proxy-bridge-ports"><span>Configure</span><strong>Proxy bridge ports</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
    <p>
      Add this library for authenticated SOCKS proxy servers:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;authenticated-socks-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      This adds no additional 3rd party dependencies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="outlook-module" class="with-back-to-top">Outlook support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="Outlook module capabilities">
    <a href="features.html#section-converting"><span>Convert</span><strong>Outlook .msg messages</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="security.html#section-reading-smime"><span>Read</span><strong>Protected Outlook messages</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
    <p>
      Add this library for parsing Outlook messages:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;outlook-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      The module delegates Outlook <code class="inline language-text">.msg</code> parsing to:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://github.com/bbottema/outlook-message-parser">org.simplejavamail:outlook-message-parser</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      That parser reads the Outlook OLE container, message properties, bodies and attachments using Apache POI and related conversion libraries.
      Its transitive dependency set belongs to the parser and can change independently of this module.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="dkim-module" class="with-back-to-top">DKIM support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="DKIM module capabilities">
    <a href="security.html#section-sending-dkim"><span>Sign</span><strong>DKIM domain signatures</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="configuration.html#section-config-mailer"><span>Reuse</span><strong>Default signing rules</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
    <p>
      Add this library for DKIM signing:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;dkim-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      This adds the following 3rd party dependencies:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://github.com/markenwerk/java-utils-mail-dkim">net.markenwerk:utils-mail-dkim</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <h2 id="smime-module" class="with-back-to-top">S/MIME support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="S/MIME module capabilities">
    <a href="security.html#section-sending-smime"><span>Protect</span><strong>Sign and encrypt messages</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="security.html#section-reading-smime"><span>Read</span><strong>Protected messages</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="features.html#section-recipient-builders"><span>Target</span><strong>Recipient certificates</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
    <p>
      Add this library for sending and reading <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S/MIME">S/MIME</a> signed or encrypted email:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;smime-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      This adds the following 3rd party dependencies:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://github.com/markenwerk/java-utils-mail-smime">net.markenwerk:utils-mail-smime</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <h2 id="cli-module" class="with-back-to-top">CLI support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="CLI module capabilities">
    <a href="cli.html#section-how-to-use"><span>Run</span><strong>Command-line sending</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="cli.html#section-at-files"><span>Reuse</span><strong>Argument files</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="cli.html#section-config-properties"><span>Configure</span><strong>Properties from the CLI</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
	  <p class="wide">
		  Normally, you would not need the CLI module on the class path, unless you wish to invoke the
		  <code class="language-text">static void main</code> method from Java (see also <a href="cli.html#navigation">how to use CLI</a>).
		  To do so, include the following module:
	  </p>
    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;cli-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      This adds the following 3rd party dependencies:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://github.com/remkop/picocli">info.picocli:picocli</a></li>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://github.com/dnault/therapi-runtime-javadoc">com.github.therapi:therapi-runtime-javadoc</a></li>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://github.com/bbottema/java-reflection">com.github.bbottema:java-reflection</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="wide">
      <strong>Note:</strong> Simple Java Mail core has a 'provided' dependency on <em>com.github.therapi:therapi-runtime-javadoc-scribe</em>
      which burns Javadoc into Simple Java Mail at compile time. This cannot be made optional. If this is a real concern, an option
      could be to provide two versions of Simple Java Mail: one with embedded Javadoc and one without (incompatible with the Cli Module).
      This is currently not in scope.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="spring-module" class="with-back-to-top">Spring support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="Spring module capabilities">
    <a href="configuration.html#section-spring-support"><span>Inject</span><strong>Spring-managed Mailer</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="configuration.html#section-config-properties"><span>Configure</span><strong>Environment and profiles</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="security.html#section-transport-strategy-oauth2"><span>Authenticate</span><strong>OAuth2 provider bean</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
	  <p>
		  Add this library for Spring support:
	  </p>
	  <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;spring-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
	  <p>
		  The module integrates with the Spring libraries supplied by your application. It expects
		  <code class="inline language-text">spring-core</code>, <code class="inline language-text">spring-context</code>, and
		  <code class="inline language-text">spring-beans</code> to be available at runtime; Spring and Spring Boot applications normally provide these already.
	  </p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="karaf-module" class="with-back-to-top">Apache Karaf support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="Karaf module capabilities">
    <a href="features.html#section-basic-usage"><span>Use</span><strong>Compose and send</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="configuration.html#section-programmatic-api-common"><span>Configure</span><strong>Build a Mailer</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
	  <p>
		  Add this library for Apache Karaf support:
	  </p>
	  <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;karaf-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
  </section>
